openai / codex conformance fixtures
===================================

Witnesses for the rules in `tests/adapter_conformance.py`, which is where the
file format is documented. In short: `rc=<int>` first, an optional
`category=<expected>` (required on every `*unavailable*` file), then
`--- stdout ---` and `--- stderr ---` sections holding raw UTF-8 bytes.

stdout here is a JSONL EVENT STREAM (`codex exec --json`), not a single
envelope: one JSON object per line, `thread.started` / `turn.started` /
`item.started` / `item.updated` / `item.completed` / `turn.completed` /
`turn.failed`, plus stream-level `{"type":"error","message":...}` lines. The
review payload rides inside the LAST `item.completed` whose `item.type` is
`agent_message`, as a JSON string in `item.text`.

The loader consumes exactly one trailing newline per section, as the separator
before the next marker. Every stream below genuinely ended with a newline on
the wire, so their stdout sections end with a blank line — that blank line is
the stream's own final newline, not formatting.

Provenance
----------

Every file marked CAPTURED came from a live `codex exec` run against
codex-cli 0.144.5 during this task's Step 0 probe, and was then sanitized.
SYNTHESIZED means written by hand. The distinction matters: a hand-written
envelope proves the adapter can parse what its author imagined, which is
exactly the thing an adapter is most likely to get wrong.

All captures were taken with `--ephemeral --ignore-user-config` and a scratch
`CODEX_HOME`, so no developer configuration, project instructions or session
files are inside these bytes.

healthy.txt
    CAPTURED, whole. A completed review of a small synthetic diff: three
    findings, `turn.completed` present, empty stderr. `line` and `category`
    are real integers/strings here. Every finding `title` is plain ASCII with
    no quotes or backslashes, which is what conformance rule 6 needs — rule 6
    splices the adapter's own stderr signal words into a title *in the raw
    bytes*, and only a title that survives `json.dumps` unchanged appears
    there verbatim.
    STRIPPED: `thread_id` (replaced by a fixed all-zero UUIDv7). The reviewed
    diff was synthetic and names nothing real; token counts are the capture's
    and identify nothing.

healthy_noisy_stderr.txt
    CAPTURED stdout + CAPTURED stderr, recombined. Rule 7's witness, and a
    second thing besides.
    The stdout is a different real review whose findings carry `"line":null`
    and `"category":null`. That is not an accident of prompting: the OpenAI
    structured-output mode the CLI's `--output-schema` feeds requires every
    property to be listed in `required`, so the adapter's strict projection
    spells the contract's OPTIONAL properties as nullable-and-required, and
    the model then answers `null` where the contract would have omitted the
    key. This capture is the proof that real bytes look like that, and the
    reason `parse` strips JSON nulls before validating.
    The stderr is the real 401 tracing output captured from the no-auth run
    below. The run must still classify `ok`, because the provider
    demonstrably served; the suite proves the fixture is not vacuous by
    classifying the SAME stderr with empty stdout and requiring
    `unavailable` there.
    STRIPPED: `thread_id`, `cf-ray` values, request ids, log timestamps.

refuter_healthy.txt
    CAPTURED, whole. A live run asked for `REFUTER_CONTRACT` — the same
    invocation with the refuter schema in `--output-schema` — answering three
    findings with `confirmed`, `confirmed`, `refuted`. It must parse under
    `REFUTER_CONTRACT` and must NOT parse under `REVIEW_CONTRACT`; a refuter
    response a Phase 1 caller could read as a review would read as a review
    with no findings, which is a clean bill of health.
    STRIPPED: `thread_id`.

degraded_stderr.txt
    CAPTURED stdout (identical to `healthy.txt`) + SYNTHESIZED stderr. The
    harness-degradation case: a valid, schema-clean stream on stdout —
    `turn.completed` and all — with stream-disconnect wording on stderr. It
    must classify `degraded`, NOT `unavailable`: usable output wins on the
    availability axis only.
    The stderr is synthesized because a mid-turn transport disconnect is not
    reproducible on demand. It is written in the tracing format the captured
    401 stderr uses, and every message string in it
    (`stream disconnected before completion`,
    `stream disconnected - retrying sampling request`,
    `stream closed before response.completed`) is present verbatim in the
    installed `codex` binary, so these are the CLI's own words rather than
    invented ones. It is also the source of the signal words rule 6 splices
    into a finding title.

degraded_truncated.txt
    CAPTURED, whole, from a run killed mid-flight: the process group was sent
    SIGKILL nine seconds in, so the stream stops after `turn.started` and the
    child reports rc -9. Events, but no terminal event and no payload — the
    plain "the run did not get to say anything" shape.
    STRIPPED: `thread_id`.

degraded_no_turn_completed.txt
    CAPTURED (`healthy.txt`'s stream) then CUT at a line boundary, dropping
    the final `turn.completed` line — exactly what a killed or timed-out run
    leaves behind once the answer is already on the wire. This is the sharp
    one: the payload is complete and schema-valid, so the run PARSES and
    reports three findings, and only the missing terminal event says the run
    never finished. It is codex's spelling of the silent false all-clear that
    116 `stopReason: Cancelled` runs produced in the Phase 1 corpus.

degraded_turn_failed.txt
    CAPTURED, whole. A real `turn.failed`: the run was launched with the
    contract's schema passed through UNPROJECTED, and the backend rejected it
    (`'additionalProperties' is required to be supplied and to be false`).
    That 400 is why the adapter projects the contract schema into strict mode
    at all, and the stream it produced is a genuine stream-level `error`
    event followed by `turn.failed`. It carries no auth, model or quota
    wording, so it must land on `degraded` rather than `unavailable`.
    STRIPPED: `thread_id`.

unavailable_auth.txt
    CAPTURED, whole, from a run with `CODEX_HOME` pointed at an empty
    directory: no credentials, rc 1, the CLI's retry ladder on both the
    WebSocket and HTTPS transports, `turn.failed`, and matching `401
    Unauthorized` tracing lines on stderr. Declares `category=auth`.
    The category is asserted EXACTLY: `auth` is attempt-local, `quota` is
    cached provider-wide, and confusing the two takes a healthy provider out
    of every later fallback chain.
    STRIPPED: `thread_id`, every `cf-ray` value, every request id, and the
    log timestamps. No token was ever in these bytes — the point of the run
    is that there was none.

unavailable_model.txt
    CAPTURED, whole, from a run asking for a model id that does not exist.
    Declares `category=model`. stderr is EMPTY here, which is the interesting
    part: the only evidence is inside the stream, in the stream-level
    `{"type":"error"}` line and in `turn.failed`'s `error.message`. Those are
    harness-authored fields; the adapter reads them and never reads
    `agent_message` text, which is what keeps review CONTENT from ever
    driving a classification.
    The model id in this file (`gpt-9.9-nonexistent`) is deliberately not a
    real one — it is the id the CLI rejected, and rejecting it is what the
    fixture witnesses. Every model id that appears in a passing invocation
    (in the tests and the example config) is one this CLI accepted live.

Sanitization
------------

These files are committed to a public repository. Every capture was checked to
contain no tokens, usernames, machine paths, home directories, repo or
project names, session ids or request ids. The reviewed material in the
healthy and refuter captures is synthetic code written for the probe, so the
finding text names nothing real. What the captures still carry is provider
constants (event type names, `api.openai.com` endpoints, HTTP status wording)
and usage numbers, which identify nothing.
